<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umiak</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as umiaq, umialak, oomiak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The umiak is an open skin boat of the Yupik and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a>, once found along all the coasts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> and first used in Thule times. Its frame of driftwood or whalebone, pegged and lashed together, was covered with walrus or bearded seal skins, about seven for a large boat of 9.1 metres. Much larger than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayak">kayak</a>, it could carry up to 15 passengers yet was light enough for a few people to carry over the ice to open water. It was used in summer to move people and their belongings to seasonal hunting grounds and to hunt whales and walrus, rowed by women or paddled by men.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umiak.jpg" alt="Umiak" width="330" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gchacon at English Wikipedia · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umiak_Anchorage_Museum_George_Chacon_26_May_2007.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
